Samsung Health Sensor SDK
-------------------------

OpenTDI contains no Samsung code. Samsung's Health Sensor SDK is optional; each user downloads it from Samsung, under
Samsung's own terms, and adds it in the app (the phone app converts it and passes it to the watch app). A developer may
instead put it in wear/libs/ for a local build (see wear/libs/README.md); such a build contains Samsung's code, and so
does a phone app built with it, inside its copy of the watch app. OpenTDI's published builds contain none:
tools/release/dexcheck.py checks every CI and release build for classes defined under com.samsung, in both APKs and in
the phone's assets/watch.apk.

The :samsung-health-stub module is original OpenTDI code that mirrors the SDK's public API (as documented in Samsung's
public API reference) so the project compiles without the SDK; it contains no Samsung code and is in neither app.


Third-party software in the apps
--------------------------------

Both apps include, under the Apache License 2.0 (https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0): AndroidX and Jetpack
Compose, including Wear Compose (The Android Open Source Project); the Kotlin standard library, kotlinx.coroutines and
kotlinx.serialization (JetBrains s.r.o. and contributors); JetBrains Java Annotations; JSpecify; and Guava's
listenablefuture (The Guava Authors). Both also include Google Play services client libraries for the watch-phone link
(play-services-wearable, -base, -basement and -tasks), which Google licenses under the Android Software Development Kit
License (https://developer.android.com/studio/terms.html), not under the GPL.

The phone app also includes:
- sherpa-onnx (Apache-2.0, https://github.com/k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx), which renders the cue voice. Its native libraries
  contain ONNX Runtime (MIT, https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime) and the espeak-ng speech engine
  (GPL-3.0-or-later, https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng), among the other components that the cmake/ folder of
  sherpa-onnx v1.13.7 lists.
- To install the watch app from the phone: Kadb (Apache-2.0, https://github.com/flyfishxu/Kadb), spake2-java (GPL-3.0,
  https://github.com/Flyfish233/spake2-java), curve25519-elisabeth and ed25519-elisabeth (MIT, with BSD-3-Clause code
  that curve25519-elisabeth ported from curve25519-dalek; https://github.com/cryptography-cafe), Bouncy Castle (MIT,
  https://www.bouncycastle.org), Okio (Apache-2.0, https://github.com/square/okio) and AndroidHiddenApiBypass
  (Apache-2.0, https://github.com/LSPosed/AndroidHiddenApiBypass).
- D8 from R8 (BSD-3-Clause, https://r8.googlesource.com/r8), which converts the user's own Samsung kit on the phone.
  R8 also carries third-party code, each part listed with its license in the LICENSE file inside r8-9.5.23.jar
  (https://dl.google.com/android/maven2/com/android/tools/r8/9.5.23/r8-9.5.23.jar).

The optional neural voice model is in neither this repository nor the apps: tools/voice/fetch_voice_model.sh downloads
Kokoro-82M (Apache-2.0) with espeak-ng data (GPL-3.0) from their publishers, and tools/voice/push_voice_model.sh
copies it to your own phone.

The optional offline transcription model is likewise in neither this repository nor the apps:
tools/stt/fetch_stt_model.sh downloads it from the sherpa-onnx project's releases, and tools/stt/push_stt_model.sh
copies it to your own phone, where the phone app runs it with sherpa-onnx (above). It is NVIDIA Parakeet TDT-CTC 110M
(https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet-tdt_ctc-110m), (c) NVIDIA, licensed under CC-BY-4.0
(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), converted to ONNX int8 by the sherpa-onnx project; with Silero VAD
(https://github.com/snakers4/silero-vad), Copyright (c) 2020-present Silero Team, MIT. The optional smaller model is
Moonshine tiny English (https://github.com/moonshine-ai/moonshine), Copyright (c) 2025 Useful Sensors, Inc. (dba
Moonshine AI), MIT.

